# Cursor Editor — Canvas System

## Overview

A Cursor Canvas is a live React application rendered beside the chat, providing rich visual presentation of analytical output. Canvases transform structured data into interactive, self-describing visual artifacts.

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "User Requests Analysis"
        A[User Request]
    end
    
    subgraph "Decision Logic"
        B{Needs visual<br/>artifact?}
        C[Use Canvas]
        D[Use Plain Text<br/>or Code Block]
    end
    
    subgraph "Canvas Creation"
        E[Write .canvas.tsx]
        F[Self-check]
        G[Present with link]
    end
    
    A --> B
    B -->|Yes| C
    B -->|No| D
    C --> E
    E --> F
    F --> G
```

---

## When to Use Canvas

**Use a canvas when the agent produces new standalone analytical output:**

| Category | Examples |
|---|---|
| Quantitative analyses | "Send 500 requests and show me failure rates", usage trend reports |
| Billing investigations | Account audits, charge breakdowns, usage cost analysis |
| Security audits | Vulnerability categorization, compliance reports |
| Architecture reviews | System dependency maps, component relationships |
| Cross-system analysis | Overlap reports, data reconciliation |
| MCP tool data | Results from Datadog, Databricks, Linear, Sentry, Slack where data IS the deliverable |
| Financial analyses | Margin decompositions, P&L breakdowns |
| Large tables | Tables with more than a handful of rows the user asked to see |
| Timelines | Project timelines, deployment history |

## When NOT to Use Canvas

| Scenario | Reason | Alternative |
|---|---|---|
| "Create a Datadog dashboard" | User wants a specific tool deliverable | Use the tool directly |
| "Draft a support response" | Targeted deliverable | Plain text in chat |
| "Fix this code" | Active development | Code changes directly |
| "Improve this HTML dashboard" | Working within existing artifact | Edit the file |
| Targeted debugging | Active debugging session | Chat explanations |
| Short factual answers | One-off questions | Plain text |
| MCP as intermediate step | Querying to draft something else | Plain text |
| Quick code edits | Single file changes | Code blocks |

